>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T ( The opacity surrounding production data has resulted in significant overproduction issues within the fashion industry. This lack of transparency directly contributes to excessive inventory accumulation and unnecessary waste generation. The solution lies in implementing data-driven production limits based on real-time sales analysis. Supporting this, the industry should transition toward made-to-order manufacturing models, facilitated by targeted financial incentives. Mandatory disclosure requirements for surplus disposal methods would create accountability for production decisions and waste management practices.)
/E ( The opacity surrounding production data has resulted in significant overproduction issues within the fashion industry. This lack of transparency directly contributes to excessive inventory accumulation and unnecessary waste generation. The solution lies in implementing data-driven production limits based on real-time sales analysis. Supporting this, the industry should transition toward made-to-order manufacturing models, facilitated by targeted financial incentives. Mandatory disclosure requirements for surplus disposal methods would create accountability for production decisions and waste management practices.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Disposal Management)
/E (Disposal Management)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Current disposal practices in the fashion industry remain largely unaccountable, with unsold inventory frequently being destroyed through burning or landfill disposal without public oversight. The implementation of Extended Producer Responsibility \(EPR\) frameworks represents a crucial step toward holding brands ac countable for their waste generation. This should be complemented by strategic regulation of textile disposal through targeted bans or taxation mechanisms. Additionally, mandatory public disclosure of waste management practices would create transparency and encourage more sustainable disposal methods.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Recycling Infrastructure)
/E (Recycling Infrastructure)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (The recycling segment of the fashion industry suffers from both a lack of transparency and insufficient collaboration among stakeholders. To address these shortcomings, the industry requires the establishment of mandatory recycling targets for brands, supported by robust monitoring and reporting systems. Significant investment in circular economy initiatives is essential to promote effective textile reuse and recycling programs. The development of stronger partnerships would enhance the efficiency. )
/E (The recycling segment of the fashion industry suffers from both a lack of transparency and insufficient collaboration among stakeholders. To address these shortcomings, the industry requires the establishment of mandatory recycling targets for brands, supported by robust monitoring and reporting systems. Significant investment in circular economy initiatives is essential to promote effective textile reuse and recycling programs. The development of stronger partnerships would enhance the efficiency. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
